      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Isolated total occlusion of the left main coronary artery is rare, and may be conventionally treated by standard bypass surgery. We describe a 54-year-old woman with isolated left main coronary artery disease presenting as unstable angina of 1 week's duration. She was treated with transaortic autologous pericardial patch angioplasty. Postoperative coronary angiography showed a widely patent left main coronary artery. She was angina-free 5 months postoperatively. In selected patients, this technique offers a valuable alternative to coronary artery bypass with the advantage of restoring more physiologic perfusion to the coronary tree."},
